  <p class="eyebrow">Guide</p>
  <h1>Assigning the month</h1>
  <p class="lede">Income waits until you give it a category. The Budget tab is where that happens.</p>

  <h2>Ready to Assign</h2>
  <p>Money that has come in and has not been given a job yet is <strong>Ready to Assign</strong>. On the Budget tab it shows as <strong>To Budget</strong>. Record income as an <strong>Inflow</strong> with the category Ready to Assign, and that amount appears here.</p>

  <h2>Categories and groups</h2>
  <p>Categories sit in groups, such as Essentials. The amount on a category is what is still available to spend. Assigning moves money from To Budget into that category. Spending in the category brings available back down.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>On iPhone, tap the category and edit <strong>Assigned</strong>.</li>
    <li>On Mac, click the category’s <strong>Assigned</strong> amount.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>You can also move available money from one category to another. Stop when To Budget is zero, or leave some there on purpose. Unassigned money stays visible.</p>
  <figure class="step-shot">
    <img src="/images/quick-start/budget.jpg" width="800" height="1740" alt="The Budget tab, with To Budget and category groups such as Essentials.">
  </figure>

  <h2>A new month</h2>
  <p>Use the month arrows to plan ahead or look back. Each month has its own assigned amounts. Money still available in a category stays in that category. It does not go back to Ready to Assign.</p>
  <p>A target changes how much the new month asks for. That is covered in <a href="/guides/targets/">Targets</a>.</p>
</article>
